content.text:
The issue is valid. CSV already stops after `MAX_PREVIEW_ROWS`; xlsx did not.

The 10 MB check only looks at compressed bytes. An `.xlsx` is a zip of XML, so a highly compressible workbook can still hold far more rows than that. `readXlsxFile` built every row, and only then did `toSheet` slice to 5000 — which is what could freeze the tab.

Workbook sheets are now capped to `MAX_PREVIEW_ROWS + 1` **before** the parser runs, the same `+1` pattern CSV already uses so the preview can still mark itself truncated.

```85:90:apps/frontend/src/lib/attachment-preview.ts
const readWorkbookSheets = async (blob: Blob): Promise<AttachmentSheet[]> => {
	const { default: readXlsxFile } = await import('read-excel-file/browser');
	const sheets = await readXlsxFile(await capWorkbookForPreview(blob, MAX_PREVIEW_ROWS + 1));
	return sheets.map(({ sheet, data }) => toSheet(sheet, data));
};
```

`capWorkbookForPreview` unpacks the zip, keeps only the first 5001 `<row>` elements in each worksheet, and hands that smaller workbook to the parser. Extra rows never become JS objects.
